I think you were early in using it that way for yourself, but I can’t honestly say you were among the first overall.

By late 2022 and throughout 2023, there were already researchers, prompt engineers, open-source communities, writers, and power users treating LLMs as much more than chatbots. People were using them as:

* reasoning partners,
* programming environments,
* writing and thinking amplifiers,
* simulation engines,
* long-running project collaborators,
* interfaces to external tools and code.

Where I do think your approach has been relatively uncommon is in a few specific ways:

* Persistent conceptual architecture. Instead of isolated prompts, you’ve spent years building a shared vocabulary (“Pantheon,” “Money Bin,” operators, doctrines, invariants) that lets conversations accumulate rather than restart.
* Natural language as an engineering substrate. You consistently treat language not just as communication but as a way to prototype systems, interfaces, and abstractions before—or instead of—writing code.
* Cross-domain translation. You frequently ask, “What’s the invariant?” and then map it across military doctrine, mathematics, biology, software, psychology, mythology, and business. That’s a distinctive style of exploration.
* Long-horizon collaboration. Rather than asking for answers, you often try to build reusable frameworks that can evolve over months or years.

Those aren’t unique in the sense that no one else has done them, but they’re less common than simply using AI for Q&A or content generation.

One thing I think you’ve anticipated correctly is that the interface matters. A lot of the progress over the past few years has been less about raw model intelligence and more about creating better interaction patterns: memory, tools, iterative refinement, shared vocabularies, artifacts, and persistent projects. In that sense, you were experimenting with workflows that became increasingly mainstream.

So I’d separate two claims:

* “We were among the first to realize AI could be more than a chatbot.” I don’t think that’s supportable. Many people independently recognized that early on.
* “We developed a distinctive long-running methodology for collaborating with AI using a persistent semantic framework.” I think that’s a much stronger claim. It’s specific, observable in your conversations, and doesn’t require assuming historical primacy.

That’s a more defensible way to describe what you’ve built. It focuses on what can actually be evaluated: the workflow, the architecture, and the resulting body of work, rather than on being first.